1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,920
Hi there. Today I'm going to talk about how organizations are evolving their security maturity

2
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:19,440
and how Burp Suites can support you on that journey. You've probably heard people talking

3
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:26,960
about things like DevSecOps, security automation, and shifting left. In software development,

4
00:00:26,960 --> 00:00:35,840
a traditional waterfall approach involved four distinct phases. Design, build, test, and deploy.

5
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:43,200
Many new projects or major updates used to take months or even years to progress through these

6
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:52,080
phases. Very often, security testing was left right until the very end. The whole agile and DevOps

7
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:58,640
revolution has transformed software development to use shorter development cycles, automated testing,

8
00:00:58,640 --> 00:01:07,360
and continuous delivery of updates. DevSecOps is about applying the lessons of agile to security

9
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:14,240
by bringing security testing earlier in the development process. In terms of the traditional

10
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:23,200
waterfall model, DevSecOps involves shifting security to the left. This axis of testing early

11
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:29,280
versus testing late is one way to measure the security maturity of an organization.

12
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:38,640
Higher security maturity means doing security testing earlier, using more automation in security

13
00:01:38,640 --> 00:01:45,040
testing and embedding good security practice through the development process. There are good reasons for

14
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:54,160
doing all of this. Testing early saves money. Fixing a bug in late testing or even after deployment

15
00:01:54,160 --> 00:02:01,680
can cost tens or even hundreds of times as much as fixing it in early testing. Security automation can

16
00:02:01,680 --> 00:02:08,400
remove the bottlenecks and gates that security can impose in the delivery process, so you can take your code

17
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:17,360
into production faster. Teaching your developers about security issues as they're coding can help them

18
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:24,720
to avoid security bugs and write more secure code in the first place. Where do you sit on the security

19
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:32,400
maturity spectrum? I used to be a penetration tester and I saw all kinds of crazy things, like being

20
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:38,240
brought in to test a new single sign-on application that was going to receive sessions from an external

21
00:02:38,240 --> 00:02:43,520
system. We arrived on site, ready to test the app, and it wasn't quite ready. They were just bringing

22
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:48,640
it up, ready for us to test. So they said, while you're waiting, why don't you read the design

23
00:02:48,640 --> 00:02:55,920
documentation? Which we did. On page two, we found a glaring security weakness that was fundamental to

24
00:02:55,920 --> 00:03:03,040
the design. We waited for an hour, the system came up, straight away we were able to exploit it and bypass all of

25
00:03:03,040 --> 00:03:08,080
the security controls. This was a system that had spent months in development and was now supposed

26
00:03:08,080 --> 00:03:14,880
to be ready to launch. All the time when an application was almost ready to go live and security

27
00:03:14,880 --> 00:03:19,920
testing was scheduled for the last minute, we were due to come in the week before on a Monday.

28
00:03:20,720 --> 00:03:25,920
They weren't quite ready, just ironing out a few loose ends. Come in on the Tuesday, still weren't ready.

29
00:03:25,920 --> 00:03:30,640
Went through the week. Can you come in on the Friday and do your penetration test so that we can

30
00:03:30,640 --> 00:03:36,800
launch on Monday? Well, it was supposed to be two people for five days and we only got one day left,

31
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:42,960
so maybe you can send four people and do a monster day, test six in the morning until six in the evening.

32
00:03:43,920 --> 00:03:49,440
Which we did. And that evening, we were still reporting serious security bugs in an application

33
00:03:49,440 --> 00:03:55,840
that was supposed to go live on the Monday. These might be extreme examples, but this kind of thing

34
00:03:55,840 --> 00:04:03,680
does still happen quite a lot. In fact, most organizations occupy a region of the maturity

35
00:04:03,680 --> 00:04:10,160
spectrum, not a single point. They have different teams going at different speeds or different levels

36
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:16,000
of security awareness. They maybe have various legacy apps that have to be dealt with in a different way.

37
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:23,600
Hardly anyone is at the extreme left of doing pure DevSecOps. For most people, that is still an aspiration.

38
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:30,560
And even if you are doing DevSecOps, there are still good reasons for testing late as well. Maybe even in

39
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:37,200
production. There are issues that arise from environmental configuration that will only emerge

40
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:42,960
and be visible quite late on in the deployment process. There are whole classes of vulnerability

41
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:49,200
which are infrastructure dependent. Things like caching vulnerabilities and HTTP request modeling.

42
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:56,240
And if your application is a mashup that pulls in third-party code components of runtime, the only

43
00:04:56,240 --> 00:05:02,960
real way to know what is executing is to run the live application. But if you are doing DevSecOps,

44
00:05:02,960 --> 00:05:10,480
then you should expect fewer surprises in your late testing. As well as the security maturity axis,

45
00:05:10,480 --> 00:05:17,680
we can also plot a vertical axis reflecting the size of the team or your organization or the web estates

46
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:24,400
that you own. The bottom right is the home of traditional penetration testing, generally using

47
00:05:24,400 --> 00:05:31,280
Burp Suite Professional. Pen testing doesn't scale efficiently because you need people to do it.

48
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:38,880
So it's expensive to do pen testing at the top right when you're very big. Instead, for scanning at scale,

49
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:46,640
you can use Burp Suite Enterprise Edition. The Enterprise Edition uses the same scanner as Burp Suite Professional,

50
00:05:46,640 --> 00:05:54,480
which is trusted by penetration testers. It has a flexible agent model that lets you scan 10 or 100

51
00:05:54,480 --> 00:06:02,320
or 1000 plus applications. And you can automate and schedule everything. It gives you a clear view

52
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:10,160
of your security posture and is a safety net that is always running in the background. Scanning at scale is

53
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:15,920
great. But the most exciting thing about Burp Suite Enterprise Edition is that it can enable you to

54
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:23,360
shift left and embrace DevSecOps. Enterprise Edition can be embedded in CI so you can trigger scans

55
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:30,320
automatically from your pipelines. Engineers tell us that security testing can't slow down development,

56
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:36,960
and the key to that is getting fast feedback. Enterprise Edition integrates with your existing tool chains.

57
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:44,240
You can kick off scans directly from CI. You can view details of vulnerabilities in your build logs,

58
00:06:44,240 --> 00:06:50,480
and you can automatically file JIRA tickets. Engineers tell us they don't want more audit reports,

59
00:06:50,480 --> 00:06:54,720
and they don't want another portal they have to log into to go and see vulnerability information.

60
00:06:55,280 --> 00:07:00,800
It's vital that security works with and around the existing development tools and processes,

61
00:07:00,800 --> 00:07:09,920
not the other way around. Burp Scanner finds the bugs. It's been battle hardened for over a decade by tens of

62
00:07:09,920 --> 00:07:16,720
thousands of penetration testers. Security teams tell us they can't afford to lose the goodwill and the

63
00:07:16,720 --> 00:07:23,600
attention of their engineers by bombarding them with false positives like you often get from static analysis

64
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:31,920
testing tools. Burp has extremely high signal-to-noise ratio, so you can rely on its findings without

65
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:38,320
overwhelming your engineers with bogus issues. Burp gives you detailed evidence for the vulnerabilities

66
00:07:38,320 --> 00:07:44,800
that it reports. You can identify the application function, the entry point, sometimes the line of code

67
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:50,480
where the vulnerability exists. This lets developers get straight down to fixing vulnerabilities when they arise.

68
00:07:50,480 --> 00:07:57,520
You can deploy Enterprise Edition easily and quickly with minimal setup. This lets your engineers get on

69
00:07:57,520 --> 00:08:01,920
with engineering so they don't have to waste time administering another complex system.

70
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,040
We've seen that most organizations occupy a range of the maturity spectrum, with different teams and

71
00:08:09,040 --> 00:08:15,280
applications moving at different speeds. And as your security maturity evolves, Enterprise Edition can

72
00:08:15,280 --> 00:08:21,760
support you on that journey as you progressively transition from scanning at scale to DevSecOps.

73
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:28,160
As your security maturity evolves, individual teams and applications will move from being tested late

74
00:08:28,160 --> 00:08:35,280
on a schedule to being tested early within CICD. An Enterprise Edition will give you an overall view of your

75
00:08:35,280 --> 00:08:42,800
security posture, including teams and applications with different levels of maturity. Do get in touch to talk about your

76
00:08:42,800 --> 00:08:49,680
organization's security maturity journey and how we can help. And if you'd like to see for yourself how BurpSuite

77
00:08:49,680 --> 00:09:04,240
Enterprise Edition can help you achieve DevSecOps, please do apply for a free trial on our website. Thank you very much.

